          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections.",
          "answer": "Repeated urine infections are common, and the reference material I found explains some of what can contribute to them. It does not say what is causing yours, and only a clinician who knows your history can work that out.\n\n**Things that can contribute**, according to the General clinical reference on urinary tract infection: anatomy (a shorter urethra in women), sexual activity, certain contraceptives including spermicides and diaphragms, changes in vaginal bacteria after menopause, not fully emptying the bladder, kidney stones, diabetes, and previous infections.\n\n**When to discuss it with a clinician:** The infectious diseases and urology guidance says a urine culture (a lab test to identify the bacteria) is used for recurrent infection, and it also lists symptoms that persist or come back within a few weeks of treatment. If you keep getting infections, that is a reasonable reason to book an appointment and ask what might be behind them.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have any of these, which the sepsis guidance lists as signs that an infection may be spreading: confusion or unusual drowsiness, very fast breathing or breathlessness, a fast heartbeat or dizziness when standing, not passing urine for many hours, shivering or feeling very cold, or a feeling of being more unwell than ever before. Call emergency services if these happen.\n\nThe reference material I searched did not cover prevention advice or treatment choices for you, and it cannot tell you which medicine, if any, is right. Those are questions for your clinician or pharmacist.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
