          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there anything that reduces how often they happen?",
          "answer": "The reference material I searched does not give a clear answer on what reduces how often urine infections happen. The General clinical reference on urinary tract infection lists factors that contribute to them, such as incomplete bladder emptying, diabetes, kidney stones, certain contraceptives, and sexual activity. It does not say which changes would lower the chance of future infections, and the diet passages I found were about blood pressure, reflux, diabetes and heart failure, not urine infections. So I cannot tell you that any diet, drink or habit will help.\n\nSome of the listed factors may be worth raising with a clinician, such as whether your bladder empties fully, whether diabetes or kidney stones have been checked, or whether a contraceptive method could be relevant. The guidance also says that repeated infections are a reason for a urine culture, so asking for one is reasonable.\n\nPrevention and treatment choices are for your clinician or pharmacist to discuss with you, based on your own health history.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you develop confusion, unusual drowsiness, fast breathing, dizziness on standing, shivering, very little urine for many hours, or a feeling of being far more unwell than usual. Call emergency services if these happen.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
