          "answer": "The reference material does not give a simple yes or no. Guidance on whether antibiotics are needed for a urine infection differs between bodies. The Infectious diseases and urology society guidance (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence) says some European guidance treats mild bladder infections with symptom relief and delayed or no antibiotics, as a shared decision with a clinician. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default, because of symptom duration and the risk of the infection reaching the kidneys. The guidance describes this as a disagreement about how much antibiotic use is worth avoiding.\n\nThe retrieved material does not say which treatment would suit you. It also does not name first-line antibiotics or explain when they are needed for a particular person. The antibiotic stewardship guidance I found is about upper respiratory infections such as colds and sore throats, not urine infections, so I have not used it here.\n\nWhether you need an antibiotic each time is a decision for a clinician who knows your history. It is reasonable to ask them about your options, including whether a urine culture would help and whether a delayed-prescription approach could be suitable for you.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have confusion, unusual drowsiness, fast breathing, dizziness on standing, shivering, very little urine for many hours, or a feeling of being far more unwell than usual. Call emergency services if these happen.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
